{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And every woman who has known a man lying with a male — kill.</b> After he began and said 'kill every male among the young,' he added again 'kill,' and both instances were necessary, the first and the second. For if the first had been said but not the second, I would understand that the verse attaches to what follows it, and that the commandment is to keep alive every woman who has known a man along with the young among the women who had not known a man; therefore it was necessary to separate between them with the word 'kill.' And if the second had been said but not the first, so that the verse would read thus: 'And now, every male among the young and every woman who has known a man lying with a male — kill,' I would understand that the commandment to kill every male among the young applies only from nine years old and upward, who is fit for intercourse, analogous to 'a woman who has known a man lying with a male'; but from nine years old and downward, who is not fit for intercourse, there is no commandment to kill him. Therefore the first instance, where it says 'kill every male among the young,' was necessary to teach that the commandment is to kill all the young among the males, even the infant lying in the cradle." "<b>And if</b> you ask and say: how did they know which of them had known a man? Thus they expounded: they would pass them before the ציץ (the frontlet), and she who had known a man — her face would turn green, and this is what they said: a sign for a forbidden relations woman is jaundice (הדרוקן). And this punishment that comes by means of the ציץ applies specifically to a forbidden relations woman (עו״א), but in the case of Israelite women — by means of barrels. Thus did the Rabbis of blessed memory expound in Yevamot, chapter הבא על יבמתו: (Judges 21:12) 'וימצאו מיושבי יבש גלעד ארבע מאות נערה בתולה אשר לא ידעו איש למשכב זכר' — how did they know? Rabbi Kahana said: they seated them at the opening of wine barrels — one who is not a virgin, her fragrance spreads; a virgin, her fragrance does not spread. And they raise an objection there: but let them pass them before the ציץ! Rav Kahana said: 'לרצון' is written (Exodus 28:38): ״והיה על מצחו תמיד לרצון להם לפני ה׳״ — 'for acceptance,' and not for punishment. If so, then in the case of Midian too? Rabbi Ashi said: 'להם' is written — 'for them' — for them for acceptance and not for punishment, but for the forbidden relations woman (עו״א), even for punishment."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {1 "עו״א read as abbreviation for ערוה/עבירה (forbidden relations woman), following the contextual contrast with בנות ישראל. The phrase 'בעולה ריחה נודף' rendered 'one who is not a virgin, her fragrance spreads,' reading בעולה as בעלה/בעולה in the sense of a non-virgin."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:27.904692319Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}